faqs:
  - q: "What makes photoelectric alarms better than the old ones?"
    a: "The old ionisation alarms are quick to notice fast, flaming fires but slow — sometimes fatally slow — on smouldering ones. Photoelectric sensors work the other way: they spot the thick smoke from a couch, curtain or charger fire early, often 20 to 30 minutes sooner. Since most fatal house fires start as slow smoulders while everyone's asleep, that's the fire you want your alarm tuned for. They're also far less touchy about cooking, so they don't get unplugged in frustration."
  - q: "What does 'long-life' or 'sealed battery' actually mean?"
    a: "The battery is built into the alarm and lasts its whole 10-year life. There's nothing to replace, so no midnight chirping and no climbing on chairs every year. When the alarm reaches the end of its life, the whole unit gets replaced — and we put a dated sticker on ours so you know exactly when that is."
  - q: "Can I just buy alarms from the hardware store and stick them up myself?"
    a: "You can, and honestly a cheap working alarm beats no alarm every time. What you're paying us for is the stuff the box doesn't tell you: which rooms need coverage, where smoke actually travels in your layout, keeping alarms out of false-alarm zones, and quality units we've had zero grief with across thousands of installs. Most of our jobs cost less than a dinner out — and they're done in under an hour."
  - q: "Do photoelectric alarms meet the rental property rules?"
    a: "Yes. Long-life photoelectric alarms are exactly what the Residential Tenancies Act calls for when alarms are replaced or added in a rental. If you're a landlord, our <a href='/rental-property-smoke-alarm-compliance/'>compliance check</a> pairs the right alarms with a written report for your file."
