        <span class="section-label">The Right Type</span>
        <h2 class="mt-2">Why Photoelectric Is the Alarm Worth Having</h2>
        <p>Here's a stat that changed how we think about this job: most fatal house fires in New Zealand don't start with a bang. They start with a smoulder — a heater too close to the washing, a phone charger under a pillow, a pot left on low. The smoke builds slowly, quietly, usually at night. And the old-style ionisation alarms that are still stuck to thousands of Christchurch ceilings are the slowest type to react to exactly that kind of fire.</p>
        <p>Photoelectric alarms use a small light beam that smoke particles scatter. Thick, slow smoke sets them off early — often while the fire is still small enough to put out with a bucket. That head start is the entire point of owning a smoke alarm.</p>
        <p>There's a second, less dramatic reason we only fit photoelectric: they don't punish you for cooking. Ionisation alarms near a kitchen go off for burnt toast, a hot pan, sometimes a decent fry-up. After the tenth false alarm, the battery comes out "just for now" — and now the house has a decoration on the ceiling instead of an alarm. Photoelectric units mostly leave your cooking alone, which means they stay working.</p>
